1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:02,160
Some stairs you climb without thinking.

2
00:00:02,160 --> 00:00:04,960
Others feel awkward, too steep or too

3
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:06,160
stretched out.

4
00:00:06,160 --> 00:00:08,000
Much of the difference comes down to two

5
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:09,760
measurements, and one rule of thumb

6
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:12,080
designers have used for over three hundred

7
00:00:12,080 --> 00:00:12,960
years.

8
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:15,040
Let's work through that rule, with a tape

9
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:17,360
measure and a little algebra.

10
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:19,200
Every step has two measurements.

11
00:00:19,360 --> 00:00:21,600
The rise is the vertical distance from one

12
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:23,040
step surface to the next.

13
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:25,680
The tread depth, on a straight stair, is

14
00:00:25,680 --> 00:00:27,440
the horizontal distance from the front

15
00:00:27,440 --> 00:00:29,200
edge of one step to the front edge of the

16
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:31,920
next. We'll call them R and T.

17
00:00:31,920 --> 00:00:34,400
On a typical comfortable home stair, the

18
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:36,400
rise is about seventeen and a half

19
00:00:36,400 --> 00:00:38,270
centimeters, and the tread depth is about

20
00:00:38,280 --> 00:00:39,120
twenty eight.

21
00:00:39,120 --> 00:00:40,880
That's about seven inches up and eleven

22
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:42,480
inches deep.

23
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:44,320
Start on flat ground.

24
00:00:44,320 --> 00:00:46,800
When you walk at an easy pace, each step

25
00:00:46,800 --> 00:00:48,880
covers some distance, from one footprint

26
00:00:48,880 --> 00:00:51,040
to the next. That distance varies from

27
00:00:51,040 --> 00:00:52,880
person to person, and with how fast you

28
00:00:52,880 --> 00:00:55,280
walk. But a stair designer needs one

29
00:00:55,280 --> 00:00:56,480
number to aim for.

30
00:00:56,550 --> 00:00:58,870
In this video, we'll use sixty three

31
00:00:58,870 --> 00:01:01,510
centimeters, about twenty five inches.

32
00:01:01,830 --> 00:01:03,590
Here's the rule of thumb.

33
00:01:03,590 --> 00:01:05,910
Twice the rise, plus the tread depth,

34
00:01:05,910 --> 00:01:07,220
should land near that target.

35
00:01:07,230 --> 00:01:09,830
Two R plus T equals about sixty three

36
00:01:09,990 --> 00:01:12,340
centimeters. The two is what makes it a

37
00:01:12,350 --> 00:01:15,110
trade off. Keep the target fixed, and add

38
00:01:15,110 --> 00:01:16,710
one centimeter to the rise.

39
00:01:16,710 --> 00:01:18,710
Then the tread depth has to shrink by two

40
00:01:18,870 --> 00:01:21,030
centimeters. Taller steps come with

41
00:01:21,030 --> 00:01:22,150
shallower treads.

42
00:01:22,150 --> 00:01:24,790
Lower steps come with deeper ones.

43
00:01:24,950 --> 00:01:26,790
This rule is old.

44
00:01:26,790 --> 00:01:28,630
In the late sixteen hundreds, the French

45
00:01:28,630 --> 00:01:30,870
architect François Blondel wrote it down

46
00:01:30,870 --> 00:01:32,790
in his course on architecture.

47
00:01:32,790 --> 00:01:35,060
His target was two French feet, about

48
00:01:35,070 --> 00:01:36,630
sixty five centimeters.

49
00:01:36,630 --> 00:01:38,630
Today, designers treat it as a starting

50
00:01:38,630 --> 00:01:40,470
point, not a law of nature.

51
00:01:40,470 --> 00:01:42,870
For our examples, we'll use sixty three,

52
00:01:42,870 --> 00:01:44,790
because real walking steps vary from

53
00:01:44,790 --> 00:01:46,150
person to person.

54
00:01:46,150 --> 00:01:48,390
Let's test it on our comfortable stair.

55
00:01:48,390 --> 00:01:49,910
The rise is seventeen and a half

56
00:01:49,990 --> 00:01:51,820
centimeters, so two rises make thirty

57
00:01:51,830 --> 00:01:53,980
five. Add the tread depth of twenty eight.

58
00:01:53,990 --> 00:01:56,620
Thirty five plus twenty eight is sixty

59
00:01:56,630 --> 00:01:59,030
three. Right on our target.

60
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:00,880
Now look at these garden steps.

61
00:02:00,880 --> 00:02:03,280
Each rise is only twelve centimeters, but

62
00:02:03,280 --> 00:02:04,990
each tread is forty five deep.

63
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:08,240
Two R plus T comes to sixty nine, well

64
00:02:08,240 --> 00:02:09,360
above our target.

65
00:02:09,360 --> 00:02:11,680
Each step reaches farther forward than an

66
00:02:11,680 --> 00:02:13,520
easy stride, so you may find yourself

67
00:02:13,520 --> 00:02:15,840
changing your pace to match the steps.

68
00:02:15,840 --> 00:02:16,880
Now the opposite.

69
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:19,190
On this steep, narrow stair, the rise is

70
00:02:19,200 --> 00:02:21,200
twenty one centimeters, and the tread

71
00:02:21,200 --> 00:02:21,830
depth is just fifteen.

72
00:02:21,840 --> 00:02:25,600
Two R plus T is fifty seven, below our

73
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:27,840
target. And a fifteen centimeter tread

74
00:02:27,840 --> 00:02:30,080
leaves much less room to place your foot.

75
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:32,800
Let's put every possible stair on one

76
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:35,760
graph. Across is the rise, and up is the

77
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:37,750
tread depth. For our target of sixty

78
00:02:37,760 --> 00:02:40,400
three, every stair sits on this straight

79
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:42,960
line: the tread depth equals sixty three

80
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:44,960
minus two times the rise.

81
00:02:44,960 --> 00:02:47,360
The slope of minus two is the trade off we

82
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:49,600
just saw. One more centimeter of rise

83
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:51,760
costs two centimeters of tread.

84
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:53,760
Our comfortable stair sits right on the

85
00:02:53,760 --> 00:02:56,160
line. The garden steps sit above it.

86
00:02:56,160 --> 00:02:58,240
The steep stair sits below it.

87
00:02:58,450 --> 00:03:00,850
But the line hides a second choice: the

88
00:03:00,850 --> 00:03:02,930
pitch, the angle of the stair.

89
00:03:02,930 --> 00:03:05,330
Two stairs can have the same Blondel value

90
00:03:05,330 --> 00:03:07,010
and very different steepness.

91
00:03:07,010 --> 00:03:09,410
The tangent of the pitch is the rise over

92
00:03:09,410 --> 00:03:10,530
the tread depth.

93
00:03:10,530 --> 00:03:12,280
A rise of fifteen with a tread of thirty

94
00:03:12,290 --> 00:03:14,530
three gives sixty three, and climbs at

95
00:03:14,530 --> 00:03:15,970
about twenty four degrees.

96
00:03:15,970 --> 00:03:17,720
A rise of twenty with a tread of twenty

97
00:03:17,730 --> 00:03:20,450
three also gives sixty three, but climbs

98
00:03:20,450 --> 00:03:22,130
at about forty one degrees.

99
00:03:22,130 --> 00:03:24,530
Same score, very different stair.

100
00:03:24,530 --> 00:03:27,010
So the rule alone can't tell you how steep

101
00:03:27,010 --> 00:03:28,370
a stair is.

102
00:03:28,370 --> 00:03:30,370
Building codes set limits too.

103
00:03:30,370 --> 00:03:32,770
In the United States, the usual limits

104
00:03:32,770 --> 00:03:34,450
come from model codes.

105
00:03:34,450 --> 00:03:37,090
Each state or city adopts them, sometimes

106
00:03:37,090 --> 00:03:38,130
with changes.

107
00:03:38,130 --> 00:03:40,130
The residential model code typically

108
00:03:40,130 --> 00:03:42,130
allows a rise of at most seven and three

109
00:03:42,290 --> 00:03:44,370
quarter inches, and a tread depth of at

110
00:03:44,370 --> 00:03:45,570
least ten inches.

111
00:03:45,570 --> 00:03:47,650
The general building code, used for most

112
00:03:47,650 --> 00:03:49,730
other buildings, allows at most seven

113
00:03:49,730 --> 00:03:51,810
inches up, and needs at least eleven

114
00:03:51,810 --> 00:03:54,450
inches deep. In England, the official

115
00:03:54,450 --> 00:03:57,330
guidance, Approved Document K, does use

116
00:03:57,330 --> 00:03:58,690
the formula directly.

117
00:03:58,690 --> 00:04:00,980
Twice the rise plus the going, their word

118
00:04:00,980 --> 00:04:02,597
for tread depth, should be between five

119
00:04:02,607 --> 00:04:04,580
hundred fifty and seven hundred

120
00:04:04,580 --> 00:04:07,140
millimeters. And a private stair in a home

121
00:04:07,220 --> 00:04:09,940
can be no steeper than forty two degrees.

122
00:04:09,940 --> 00:04:11,860
There's one more rule, and the reason

123
00:04:11,860 --> 00:04:14,020
behind it has been measured in a lab.

124
00:04:14,020 --> 00:04:16,180
In a twenty twenty study, researchers

125
00:04:16,180 --> 00:04:18,820
built a test staircase and made one riser

126
00:04:18,820 --> 00:04:20,980
just ten millimeters higher than the rest.

127
00:04:21,140 --> 00:04:23,540
Going up, people's feet cleared that step

128
00:04:23,540 --> 00:04:25,620
by about nine millimeters less.

129
00:04:25,620 --> 00:04:27,380
Less clearance can increase the risk of

130
00:04:27,380 --> 00:04:29,140
catching a toe and tripping.

131
00:04:29,140 --> 00:04:31,140
That's why the American residential model

132
00:04:31,300 --> 00:04:32,980
code limits the difference between the

133
00:04:32,980 --> 00:04:35,380
tallest and shortest riser in one flight

134
00:04:35,380 --> 00:04:36,900
to three eighths of an inch.

135
00:04:36,900 --> 00:04:39,300
That's about nine and a half millimeters.

136
00:04:39,300 --> 00:04:41,700
Now let's design a straight staircase.

137
00:04:41,700 --> 00:04:43,370
The height from floor to floor is two

138
00:04:43,380 --> 00:04:45,140
hundred seventy centimeters.

139
00:04:45,140 --> 00:04:47,140
We'd like a rise near seventeen and a

140
00:04:47,140 --> 00:04:49,700
half. Two seventy divided by seventeen and

141
00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:52,100
a half is about fifteen point four.

142
00:04:52,100 --> 00:04:54,420
We can't build part of a step, so we round

143
00:04:54,420 --> 00:04:55,860
to fifteen risers.

144
00:04:55,860 --> 00:04:58,020
Now every rise is two seventy over

145
00:04:58,020 --> 00:05:00,580
fifteen: exactly eighteen centimeters.

146
00:05:00,890 --> 00:05:03,450
The rule of thumb gives the tread depth.

147
00:05:03,450 --> 00:05:05,850
Two times eighteen is thirty six.

148
00:05:05,850 --> 00:05:08,330
Sixty three minus thirty six leaves twenty

149
00:05:08,330 --> 00:05:09,690
seven centimeters.

150
00:05:09,690 --> 00:05:12,650
Fifteen risers need only fourteen treads,

151
00:05:12,650 --> 00:05:14,650
because the last step is the upper floor

152
00:05:14,650 --> 00:05:17,370
itself. Fourteen times twenty seven is

153
00:05:17,370 --> 00:05:19,530
three hundred seventy eight centimeters.

154
00:05:19,530 --> 00:05:21,770
So the flight itself covers a horizontal

155
00:05:21,770 --> 00:05:24,490
run of about three point eight meters, at

156
00:05:24,490 --> 00:05:27,050
a pitch of about thirty four degrees.

157
00:05:27,050 --> 00:05:29,450
Now check two residential model code

158
00:05:29,450 --> 00:05:31,547
dimensions. Eighteen centimeters is seven

159
00:05:31,557 --> 00:05:34,010
point one inches, under seven and three

160
00:05:34,090 --> 00:05:36,507
quarters. Twenty seven centimeters is ten

161
00:05:36,517 --> 00:05:39,130
point six inches, more than ten.

162
00:05:39,130 --> 00:05:41,850
Both pass. A complete design still has to

163
00:05:41,850 --> 00:05:43,530
account for landings, headroom and

164
00:05:43,530 --> 00:05:44,810
handrails.

165
00:05:44,810 --> 00:05:47,370
So why do some stairs feel easy, while

166
00:05:47,370 --> 00:05:48,810
others feel awkward?

167
00:05:48,810 --> 00:05:51,050
Two measurements tell much of the story.

168
00:05:51,050 --> 00:05:53,530
Twice the rise plus the tread depth gives

169
00:05:53,530 --> 00:05:56,090
a quick check, and our target was sixty

170
00:05:56,090 --> 00:05:57,130
three centimeters.

171
00:05:57,440 --> 00:05:59,920
Every extra centimeter of rise costs two

172
00:05:59,920 --> 00:06:01,440
centimeters of tread.

173
00:06:01,440 --> 00:06:03,440
The same score can still mean a gentle

174
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:06,240
stair or a steep one, so check the pitch

175
00:06:06,240 --> 00:06:09,360
too. And keep every step the same.

176
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:11,600
Next time a staircase feels strange, take

177
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:13,040
out a tape measure and see what the

178
00:06:13,040 --> 00:06:13,840
numbers say.

179
00:06:14,080 --> 00:06:15,920
If you enjoyed this one, please give it a

180
00:06:15,920 --> 00:06:17,680
like and subscribe for more everyday

181
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:19,280
engineering. Thanks for watching!
